    {
      "id": "r4-factor-cubic",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "skills": [
        "quad-rational"
      ],
      "prose": "Factorise $x^3 - 4x^2 + x + 6$ fully. Try the factors of 6.",
      "question": "Type the factorised form.",
      "widget": {
        "kind": "expression",
        "answer": "(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)",
        "accept": [
          "(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)"
        ],
        "form": "factorised-fully",
        "wrong": [
          {
            "id": "signs",
            "tex": "(x-1)(x+2)(x+3)"
          }
        ],
        "keyboard": "algebra"
      },
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "$f(-1) = f(2) = f(3) = 0$, so it is $(x + 1)(x - 2)(x - 3)$.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Find the roots among $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6$, then write a bracket for each.",
        "byKey": {
          "signs": "Each bracket has the opposite sign to its root: the roots are $-1$, 2 and 3.",
          "form": "Factorise fully: the quadratic factor still splits into two brackets."
        }
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "$f(-1) = -1 - 4 - 1 + 6 = 0$, so $(x + 1)$ is a factor, and dividing leaves $x^2 - 5x + 6 = (x - 2)(x - 3)$. So $x^3 - 4x^2 + x + 6 = (x + 1)(x - 2)(x - 3)$."
      }
    },
